<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigonid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Antigonid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Antigonid dynasty was a Hellenistic dynasty descended from Alexander's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>. It ruled Macedonia from the third century BCE until the Roman conquest in 168 BCE. It was one of the main successor states that emerged from the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Antigonid dynasty" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perseus_of_Macedonia_Tetradrachm_90060057.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Antigonid dynasty was a Macedonian Greek royal house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>, a general and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who first came to power in the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>. It was one of four dynasties established by Alexander's successors, the others being the Seleucid, Ptolemaic and Antipatrid dynasties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> had become extinct when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> ordered the assassination, in 310 or 309 BCE, of the 14-year-old Alexander IV, son of Alexander the Great, and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxane</a>, and the Antigonid and Antipatrid dynasties, the latter led by Cassander, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>, sought to fill the vacuum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 307 BCE Demetrius, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, ousted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>'s governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum">Demetrius of Phalerum</a>, and after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> at the Battle of Salamis in 306 BCE he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Antigonus then took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>, king, from the assembled armies, and ruled territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, sometimes called the Antigonid Empire. A revived coalition of Cassander, Ptolemy I Soter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> decisively defeated the Antigonids at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE, where Antigonus was killed.</p><p>Demetrius, who is known as Poliorcetes, survived, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and in 294 BCE made himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. In 288 BCE Pyrrhus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> drove him out, and he died a prisoner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>. After a long period of instability his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> established the family's control over Macedon and over most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> by 276 BCE, despite the loss of the Asian territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> extended Macedonian influence in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and re-established the Hellenic Alliance with himself as leader. Under Philip V, Macedon first came into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, already a decisive power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The last Antigonid king, Perseus, who reigned from 179 to 168 BCE, became known as the champion of Greek resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but he could not stop the Roman legions. His defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Battle of Pydna</a> in 168 BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a>, ended the dynasty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> came under the control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. A rebel against Rome, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a>, claimed to be a son of Perseus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antigonid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Antigonid dynasty</b> began not in Macedon but in Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ptolemaic, Seleucid and <b>Antigonid</b> kingdoms inherited and adapted the Macedonian model, which combined military force, economic control and cultural prestige.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
